in 1858, the European population of the provincial district was only 1,185.","wpu":"Hawke's Bay Province","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Hawke%27s_Bay_in_New_Zealand_%281873%29.svg/960px-Hawke%27s_Bay_in_New_Zealand_%281873%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Napier Hill","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.479749,"lo":176.910528,"d":556,"ds":"Napier Hill (Māori: Mataruahou) is a limestone outcrop and suburb rising above the lowland districts of the city of Napier on New Zealand's North Island. The north-east end, Bluff Hill, has a steep cliff face overlooking the Port of Napier. It features Napier Girls' High School, the historic former Napier Prison and a scenic walk.","wpu":"Napier Hill","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Port_of_Napier_as_seen_from_Bluff_Hill_Lookout_%2814757349814%29.jpg/960px-Port_of_Napier_as_seen_from_Bluff_Hill_Lookout_%2814757349814%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Napier Port","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.4748,"lo":176.9191,"d":605,"ds":"Napier Port is in Napier, New Zealand, on Hawke Bay. It is the North Island's second largest export port by tonnage. It is owned by Napier Port Holdings Limited (NZX: NPH), which is 55% owned by Hawke's Bay Regional Council through its wholly owned subsidiary Hawke's Bay Regional Investment Company, as of November 2024.","wpu":"Napier Port","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Port_of_Napier.JPG/960px-Port_of_Napier.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Clive Square","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.491944,"lo":176.913611,"d":1359,"ds":"Clive Square is a central city park and recreation space in the city of Napier, New Zealand.","wpu":"Clive Square","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Clive_Square_-_Napier%2C_NZ_-_DSC05236.jpg/960px-Clive_Square_-_Napier%2C_NZ_-_DSC05236.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Anglican Diocese of Waiapu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.4944,"lo":176.9165,"d":1601,"ds":"The Diocese of Waiapu is one of the 13 dioceses and hui amorangi (Māori bishoprics) of the Anglican Church in Aotearoa, New Zealand and Polynesia. The Diocese covers the area around the East Coast of the North Island of New Zealand, including Tauranga, Taupō, Gisborne, Hastings and Napier. It is named for the Waiapu River.","wpu":"Anglican Diocese of Waiapu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Angl-NZ-Waiapu-Arms.svg/960px-Angl-NZ-Waiapu-Arms.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Marineland of New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.494662,"lo":176.919161,"d":1640,"ds":"Marineland of New Zealand was a marine mammal park in Napier, New Zealand, that opened in 1965 and closed to the public in 2008. It housed several species of native and introduced marine wildlife, most notably common dolphins.","wpu":"Marineland of New Zealand","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Entrance_Marineland.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ahuriri","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.481459,"lo":176.89796,"d":1642,"ds":"Ahuriri is a suburb of the city of Napier, in the Hawke's Bay region of New Zealand's eastern North Island. The area was a major site of Māori and European settlement, and the site of the Port of Napier until the 1931 Hawke's Bay earthquake. It has since been redeveloped as a mixed commercial and residential area.","wpu":"Ahuriri","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Ahuriri_estuary_bridges_from_Bluff_Hill_Lookout%2C_Napier%2C_March_2026.jpg/960px-Ahuriri_estuary_bridges_from_Bluff_Hill_Lookout%2C_Napier%2C_March_2026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Marine Parade, Napier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.5,"lo":176.918611,"d":2228,"ds":"Marine Parade is a major thoroughfare, combining roadway and esplanade, running along the foreshore in the city of Napier, on Hawke Bay in the North Island of New Zealand.","wpu":"Marine Parade, Napier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/View_down_Marine_Parade%2C_Napier%2C_July_2025_%281%29.jpg/960px-View_down_Marine_Parade%2C_Napier%2C_July_2025_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Napier South","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.5,"lo":176.913,"d":2250,"ds":"Napier South is a suburb of the city of Napier, in the Hawke's Bay region of New Zealand's eastern North Island. For some purposes, such as postal, it extends to include the Napier Central statistical area (the central business district), which is covered here at Central Napier.","wpu":"Napier South","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/McLean_Park%2C_Napier.jpg/960px-McLean_Park%2C_Napier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Aquarium of New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.5008,"lo":176.9189,"d":2318,"ds":"The National Aquarium of New Zealand, formerly Napier Aquarium, is a public aquarium on Marine Parade in Napier, New Zealand. It was started in 1957 and moved to its present location in December 1976. It is owned by Napier City Council.","wpu":"National Aquarium of New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Napier_aquarium_entrance.jpg/960px-Napier_aquarium_entrance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"McLean Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.501944,"lo":176.912778,"d":2466,"ds":"McLean Park is a sports ground in Napier, New Zealand. The two main sports played at the ground are cricket and rugby union. It is one of the largest cricket grounds in New Zealand.","wpu":"McLean Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/McLean_Park%2C_Napier.jpg/960px-McLean_Park%2C_Napier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Westshore, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.4778,"lo":176.8823,"d":2988,"ds":"Westshore is a northern coastal suburb of the city of Napier in Hawke's Bay, New Zealand.","wpu":"Westshore, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Westshore_Beach_Reserve%2C_Napier.jpg/960px-Westshore_Beach_Reserve%2C_Napier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Onekawa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.5084,"lo":176.8902,"d":3906,"ds":"Onekawa is a suburb of the city of Napier, in the Hawke's Bay Region of the eastern North Island of New Zealand. Development of the suburb began in the late 1940s, after the land was acquired from the Harbour Board. The New Zealand Ministry for Culture and Heritage gives a translation of \"salty soil\" for Onekawa.","wpu":"Onekawa","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Onekawa_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ahuriri Lagoon","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.466667,"lo":176.866667,"d":4567,"ds":"Ahuriri Lagoon (Māori: Te Whanganui-a-Orotū) was a large tidal lagoon at Napier, on the east coast of New Zealand's North Island, that largely drained when the area was raised by the 1931 Hawke's Bay earthquake. Before the earthquake, the lagoon stretched several kilometres from north to south, and covered an area of roughly 3,840 ha (9,500 acres).","wpu":"Ahuriri Lagoon","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Port_Ahuriri%2C_Napier_%2821689644395%29.jpg/960px-Port_Ahuriri%2C_Napier_%2821689644395%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Poraiti","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.504273,"lo":176.843913,"d":6827,"ds":"Poraiti is a suburb on the western outskirts of the city of Napier, in the Hawke's Bay region of New Zealand's eastern North Island. For a long time it was a semi-rural locality on low hills, but has now been extended to include a new residential subdivision developed on the plain in the 2000s.","wpu":"Poraiti","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Sunset_over_Poraiti_Hills.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Greenmeadows, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.526003,"lo":176.861586,"d":6983,"ds":"Greenmeadows is a suburb of the city of Napier, in the Hawke's Bay region of New Zealand's eastern North Island. It is part of the Taradale ward of Napier and was part of the earlier Taradale Borough.","wpu":"Greenmeadows, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Anderson_Park%2C_Napier.jpg/960px-Anderson_Park%2C_Napier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Meeanee, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.544,"lo":176.893,"d":7408,"ds":"Meeanee is a locality south of the city of Napier, in the Hawke's Bay region on the east coast of New Zealand's North Island. It was named after the Battle of Meeanee (now spelled Miani) in India, won by Sir Charles Napier, the city's namesake. It is one of several places in Hawke's Bay, including Clive and Havelock North, that are named after events or people in Colonial India.","wpu":"Meeanee, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/St_Marys_Church%2C_Taradale._Now_a_restaurant_called_The_Church%2C_Meeanee.JPG/960px-St_Marys_Church%2C_Taradale._Now_a_restaurant_called_The_Church%2C_Meeanee.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bay View, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.428,"lo":176.857,"d":7744,"ds":"Bay View, previously known as Kai-arero and Petane, is a settlement in the Hawke's Bay region of the eastern North Island of New Zealand. It lies on State Highway 2, nine kilometres north of the city centre of Napier. The Esk River flows into the sea just to the north.","wpu":"Bay View, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Napier_from_Bay_View_beach%2C_January_2026.jpg/960px-Napier_from_Bay_View_beach%2C_January_2026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Taradale, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.533333,"lo":176.85,"d":8258,"ds":"Taradale is a residential suburb of the city of Napier in the Hawke's Bay region of the North Island of New Zealand. It is located 10 kilometres south-west of the centre of Napier. For hundreds of years, hills overlooking what is now Taradale were the site of villages occupied by Māori people, latterly of the Ngāti Kahungunu tribe.","wpu":"Taradale, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Sugar_Loaf_Hill%2C_Taradale_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Sugar_Loaf_Hill%2C_Taradale_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Esk River (Hawke's Bay)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.3978,"lo":176.885,"d":9544,"ds":"The Esk River of Hawke's Bay, in the eastern North Island of New Zealand, one of two rivers of that name in the country, is one of Hawke's Bay's major rivers. It flows south from the Maungaharuru Range before turning east to reach Hawke Bay 10 kilometres (6 mi) north of Napier. State Highway 5 follows the lower course of the river for several kilometres close to the settlement of Eskdale.","wpu":"Esk River (Hawke's Bay)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Esk_River_mouth%2C_Hawke%27s_Bay_PA02.jpg/960px-Esk_River_mouth%2C_Hawke%27s_Bay_PA02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ngaruroro River","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-39.56944,"lo":176.92916,"d":9999,"ds":"The Ngaruroro River is located in the eastern North Island of New Zealand. It runs for a total of 164 km (102 mi) southeast from the Kaweka Range, Kaimanawa Range and Ruahine Range and then east before emptying into Hawke Bay roughly halfway between the cities of Napier and Hastings, near the town of Clive (drainage area 2,000 square kilometres (770 sq mi) above Tūtaekurī River confluence).","wpu":"Ngaruroro River","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Ngaruroro_River_at_Kuripapango_2.jpg/960px-Ngaruroro_River_at_Kuripapango_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Napier ( NAY-pee-ər; Māori: Ahuriri) is a city in the Hawke's Bay region on the eastern coast of the North Island of New Zealand. It is a beachside city with a seaport, known for its sunny climate, esplanade lined with Norfolk pines, and extensive Art Deco architecture. For these attributes, Napier is sometimes romantically referred to as the \"Nice of the Pacific\".","wpu":"Napier, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/T_%26_G_building_and_Masonic_Hotel%2C_Napier%2C_September_2026.jpg/960px-T_%26_G_building_and_Masonic_Hotel%2C_Napier%2C_September_2026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}